description:
crate-jmap-server/src/backend.rs:429-433 — SetErrorType::serialize is:

  impl serde::Serialize for SetErrorType {
      fn serialize<S: serde::Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
          s.serialize_str(&self.to_string())
      }
  }

Every serialization allocates a fresh `String` via Display, then passes a borrow of that String to `serialize_str`. The String is dropped immediately after.

For all the typed variants (23 of 24, all branches except `Custom(s)`), `Display` writes a static `&'static str` literal via `f.write_str(s)`. The Display path could feed `serialize_str` directly with no allocation. The current shape allocates on every wire-format SetError serialize, of which there is roughly one per failing-row in every `/set` response — i.e. quite a lot.

A serious Rust practitioner reads `s.serialize_str(&self.to_string())` and reaches for `Serializer::collect_str(self)`, which serde provides specifically for this pattern:

  impl serde::Serialize for SetErrorType {
      fn serialize<S: serde::Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
          s.collect_str(self)
      }
  }

`collect_str` takes any `T: Display`, calls `Display::fmt` into a writer that the serializer chooses (often a stack buffer for short strings), and never allocates a heap `String`. For 24 of the 24 SetErrorType variants the result is identical bytes-on-the-wire with zero allocations.

Alternative shape (slightly more code, but transparent):

  impl serde::Serialize for SetErrorType {
      fn serialize<S: serde::Serializer>(&self, s: S) -> Result<S::Ok, S::Error> {
          let lit: &str = match self {
              Self::Forbidden => "forbidden",
              // ... 22 more arms identical to the Display match ...
              Self::Custom(s) => s.as_str(),
          };
          s.serialize_str(lit)
      }
  }

This duplicates the Display table (also flagged in the workspace AGENTS.md "no strum / serde_with" decision) but is mechanically the same cost as the existing Display arm and removes the per-call allocation entirely. The `set_error_type_all_known_variants_round_trip` test already proves Display and Deserialize agree; a per-arm Serialize would join the same drift-detection coverage.

This is a clippy-class finding (P2 chore, not a P1 idiom defect) because the cost is per-call allocation rather than wrong behaviour. But it is the exact pattern that an experienced Rust reviewer flags on sight — `serialize_str(&self.to_string())` is the standard "I forgot serde has collect_str" smell.

Fix is one line:

  -        s.serialize_str(&self.to_string())
  +        s.collect_str(self)

No test changes required; the existing round-trip and Custom-variant tests cover the wire-format invariant.
